llvm-4.0.0 backport patch: BasicAA does not call two positive-offset GEPs no-alias (PS4)

This patch CHANGES CODE GENERATION where alias analysis lets a load be
forwarded across a store through a variable index. BasicAA's rule that a
GEP whose variable indices are all known positive cannot alias an object
it starts beyond was added for 3.6 (r221876), reverted for 3.7 (r245394,
llvm-project b700e357b5ca) and reapplied for 3.8 (r246502, 5989bc6f4161,
2015-08-31). The base reloads a count after a store to entries[count]
(0x13883d0: equal in the prebuilt 3.7.1's module, first lost in 3.8.1's,
at the first InstCombine, which forwards the load). With the rule's use in
aliasGEP taken back that function compiles to the base's bytes and is the
only one of the module that changes; no matched game row changes.

diff -ru a/lib/Analysis/BasicAliasAnalysis.cpp b/lib/Analysis/BasicAliasAnalysis.cpp
--- a/lib/Analysis/BasicAliasAnalysis.cpp
+++ b/lib/Analysis/BasicAliasAnalysis.cpp
@@ -1246,7 +1246,9 @@
 
   if (!DecompGEP1.VarIndices.empty()) {
     uint64_t Modulo = 0;
-    bool AllPositive = true;
+    // PS4: no NoAlias from variable indices known non-negative, as in 3.7.1 (r245394 reverted
+    // r221876 on the 3.7 branch; r246502, first in 3.8, reapplied it): not taken.
+    bool AllPositive = false;
     for (unsigned i = 0, e = DecompGEP1.VarIndices.size(); i != e; ++i) {
 
       // Try to distinguish something like &A[i][1] against &A[42][0].
